    "summary": "High‑energy Good Lovin’ > China Cat sandwich and tight early‑'72 set",
    "blurb": "The show shines with a monster Good Lovin’ → China Cat Sunflower → Good Lovin’ sandwich, plus standout Jack Straw and Loser. The second set delivers a blistering Not Fade Away → Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad sequence and a rousing One More Saturday Night. Soundboard recordings capture the band in crisp, classic form.",
    "review": "What unfolds at Winterland on January 2, 1972 finds the Dead locked in early‑'72 stride. Jerry’s sparkling guitar tones soar through the iconic Good Lovin’ > China Cat Sunflower > Good Lovin’ sandwich, while his vocal work on Ramble On Rose and Casey Jones adds extra punch. Phil lays down a solid bass foundation that really lifts Loser and fuels the high‑tempo Not Fade Away → Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad medley. Keith’s piano tinkles in Mixolydian color on China Cat and provides tasteful texture throughout, and Bill (with occasional Mickey fire) drives a tight, two‑drummer groove that keeps the momentum blazing. Bob Weir adds his characteristic swagger on One More Saturday Night and steadies the rhythm guitar duties across the set. The band’s energy stays high from the opening Truckin’ through the encore, delivering classic arrangements with crisp interplay and few missteps – an excellent snapshot of the Dead at a pivotal moment in their evolution.",
